 private static final ThreadLocal<SecurityContext> contextHolder = new ThreadLocal<>();

 public static SecurityContext getContext() {
     SecurityContext ctx = contextHolder.get();
     if (ctx == null) {
         ctx = createEmptyContext();
         contextHolder.set(ctx);
     }
     return ctx;
 }
 Once the request is processed, Spring clears the security context to prevent leaks.
 4. What Happens in Subsequent Requests?
 The client sends JSESSIONID (session token) in each request.
 SecurityContextPersistenceFilter restores the security context from the session.
 Authentication is reloaded into SecurityContextHolder.getContext().
 The request is processed using the authenticated user.